course: dictionary
lesson: 1844
title: One bird, many names
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A cockerel is a young male chicken. Farm stories often use this word. In them, the cockerel crows «cock-a-doodle-doo» at dawn to wake everyone up, so the words around it show you what it does.
explain: In everyday speech, people also use cockerel for any male chicken, even one that is not young. In the USA and Australia, the same bird is usually called a rooster, so rooster means the same thing there.
explain: Science helps too. A hen lays eggs, but a cockerel does not. «A hen lays eggs whether or not there is a cockerel» tells you that laying eggs is the hen's job, not the cockerel's.
fact: A cockerel is a young male chicken.
fact: In everyday speech, «cockerel» can mean any male chicken, and this is also called a rooster.
fact: In the USA and Australia, people more often say rooster for the same bird.
fact: A hen lays about one egg a day, with or without a cockerel.
fact: Some farm animals, like cockerels and geese, guard their patch: stay calm, keep something between you and them, and let an adult deal with them.
exercise: «The cockerel crowed at dawn to wake the farm.» What is a cockerel? | *A young male chicken | A cow that lives on a farm | A hen that lays eggs | A sheep with a long tail
exercise: «In the USA, people often say rooster for the same bird.» What is a rooster? | *A male chicken | A young horse | A female chicken that lays eggs
exercise: «A hen lays eggs whether or not there is a cockerel.» Which animal lays the eggs? | *A hen | A cow | A horse | A cockerel
test: «Some farm animals, like cockerels and geese, guard their patch.» If a cockerel is guarding its patch, what should you do? | Feed it all your sandwiches | *Stay calm, keep something between you and it, and let an adult deal with it | Run at it waving your arms | Pick it up and carry it away
test: «The first hot-air balloon passengers, in 1783, were a sheep, a duck and a cockerel.» Which animal was NOT one of the first passengers? | *A hen | A sheep | A cockerel | A duck
exam: Mia says a cockerel lays the eggs you buy in the supermarket. What is the right answer? | Yes, cockerels lay the eggs and hens only crow | *No, a hen lays eggs, and most shop eggs were never fertilised, so they have no chick inside | No, eggs come from cows | Yes, cockerels lay an egg every morning
